marco: Quick one - customer sees 'Request is not a constructor' when using our SDK inside a Vercel Edge function. They also installed a node-fetch polyfill at app-level. Any concise recommended guidance?

anya: Short answer: do not polyfill globals for edge runtimes. Those runtimes already ship web fetch + Request + Response and polyfills create constructor identity collisions.

trent: From an SDK angle we added two helpers that cut down on support tickets: getGlobalFetch() (sync, no dynamic imports) and resolveFetchAsync() (async, will import undici only at runtime in Node). Docs show both patterns.

lucy: Example snippets we ship in README:

```js
// sync (safe to import in bundles)
export function getGlobalFetch() {
  return typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' ? globalThis.fetch : undefined;
}

// async (will import undici in node when needed)
export async function resolveFetchAsync() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  const mod = await import('undici');
  return mod.fetch;
}
```

marco: Nice. TypeScript problems show up too - duplicates of lib.dom and undici types. What did we settle on for public d.ts?

anya: Keep public types minimal. We export a BasicFetch alias in our types: `export type BasicFetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;` and accept ClientOptions.fetch?: BasicFetch. That prevents undici types leaking into our package typings.

trent: Also recommend duck-typing checks inside the SDK instead of instanceof. Example util: `isFetchCompatible(f)` that verifies it's a function and can be called, and `hasReadableStream(res)` to check streaming support (res?.body?.getReader). That reduces Vite/webpack polyfill breakage.

lucy: For packaging we added a small exports map so CJS consumers don't get ERR_REQUIRE_ESM. Example mapping in package.json: 

```json
{"exports":{"import":"./dist/esm/index.js","require":"./dist/cjs/index.js"},"main":"./dist/cjs/index.js","module":"./dist/esm/index.js"}
```

marco: Good. Anything specific for onboarding docs / sample-apps to save time?

anya: Yes - three tiny recipes in README/sample-apps: Next.js Edge (TSX) showing `new Client({ fetch: globalThis.fetch })`; Vite SPA note to disable node polyfills or alias them away; Node CJS example showing `const { fetch } = require('undici'); const client = new Client({ fetch });`. Link to FAQ: "Edge errors: Request is not a constructor / streaming missing".

trent: Also a small CI smoke job we added to the fetch-adapter repo: run the ESM bundle in a Cloudflare Workers emulation, run node20 without undici, and run node18/undici. That caught an edge-case where dynamic import was inlined by a bundler.

lucy: Final pragmatic tip: if a user still sees issues, ask them to grep for "polyfill-global" or "whatwg-fetch" in their deps and remove or scope it only to node builds. We added a tiny diagnostic script to sample-apps: it detects multiple Request constructors and prints guidance.

marco: Perfect. I'll add this to the SDK troubleshooting section and open a short PR in sample-apps with the three recipes & a diagnostic script.

